  "query_preview": "..A typical pattern for early July has developed across \nArizona. A strong ridge in place over eastern Arizona and much of \nNew Mexico will keep many areas hot, dry and breezy. However, this \nridge has also allowed for a thermal low to form along the Lower \nColorado Rover, with Monsoonal moisture now pooling throughout \nnorthern Mexico, western New Mexico and southeastern Arizona. This \nmoisture will find elevated heat sources to develop convection over \nthe next few days, while battling drier and more stable air over \nwestern and northwestern Arizona.\n\nThe ridge then flattens a bit later in the week, likely pushing any \nconvection south of our CWA for a few days, before rebounding by \nnext weekend and into early next week. This pattern, out around the \n6 to 7 day range, is looking quite a bit like the North American \nMonsoon, let us hope.\n\nBefore we get to that point, most of the region will continue to \nroast this week, especially the western sections. Temperatures at \nthe lower elevations will flirt with Extreme Heat for most of the \nweek and products are out along the Colorado River. Expect normal \nsouthwesterly breezes each afternoon, nothing that looks like any \nheadlines will be needed.\n\nOver eastern Arizona, expect convection to develop over the White \nMountains for the next few afternoons, possibly as far west as \nFlagstaff by Tuesday. This is largely due to outflows driving \nmoisture westward and against the prevailing flow.",
